this video answers the homework  questions I said in my previous video  called understanding quantum mechanics  if you're new to my channel you probably  think it's extremely naive of me to set  homework questions  no one's obliged to do on YouTube videos  that no one's obliged to watch and it  probably is naive but I just don't  believe that learning is a passive  process even if you never do answer the  homework questions I hope that me asking  them prompts you to think and I hope  that this video will be useful to you  because it'll cover the common  misconceptions from the original video  and I'll also answer the most frequently  asked questions  okay so the first time went question was  can you do the double slit experiment  with large objects and still get an  interference pattern  remember in that video I said that  objects only act strangely when they're  not being measured  but this doesn't happen for a big object  because it's constantly being measured  so the straightforward answer to this  question is just stop things from  measuring it  but is that possible people pointed out  that if an object is made out of lots of  particles wouldn't the different  particles interact with each other  and what about gravity every object is  interacting with other objects via  gravity and so doesn't that mean that  nothing can escape being measured  there were lots of great questions along  these lines and somehow they all boil  down to this bigger question what is a  measurement this is easily the  million-dollar question in quantum  mechanics and it's still unsolved and so  we're going to talk about it in lots and  lots of detail in the main series but  for now I want to address a  misconception that I can debunk that I  saw in the comments a lot people thought  that when I said measurement what I  really meant was interaction in other  words they thought that if a particle  interacts with another object it has  necessarily measured it and I can see  why you would think this since all my  examples of measurements were about  particles hitting other particles but  this is not necessarily the case  actually a measurement is when you get  information about what the particle is  doing leaking out let me explain  in one example that I showed the object  is hit with an air molecule but the  point is the air molecule will change  its own path depending on where the  object is if it's here it'll bounce off  like this but if it's here then the air  bounces off like this and so since us  seeing the final state of the air  molecule is enough to decide where the  object is we say that the air molecule  is carrying away information about the  object's position  and that's what makes this a measurement  not the fact that the air is interacting  with the object but that it's leaking  information about the object to the  outside world  let me explain this point a little bit  further with another example suppose  that you have some weird hypothetical  thing that changes color when it comes  into contact with our object but it goes  straight through it this is clearly some  sort of weird interaction now say the  object can be either here or here again  and the thing comes in like this in  either case the final state of the thing  is this the thing clearly interacted  with the object but it hasn't measured  it since it gives you no information  about where the object is that's an  important point so I hope it's a bit  clearer now now let me get back to the  question about when our object is made  of different interacting parts both  simplicity imagine our object is just  made out of two bits stuck together if  one of the bits is in this position then  you know that the other bit has to be  here too  and similarly if you know one part is  over here then the other bit would be  there too and so clearly this one bit  does contain information about the other  the reason this doesn't count as a  measurement though is that this  information isn't being leaked out to  the wider world and so for it to count  as a measurement in quantum mechanics  it's really important that the  information be carried away and you can  no longer interact the two particles  together there's a reason for this and  it's something I'll cover in detail in  the main series but for now let me  convince you by appealing to experiment  the double slit experiment has been done  with many things made of of composite  parts many of you mentioned they've done  it with buckyballs which are molecules  with 60 carbon atoms and still saw the  interference pattern but apparently it's  been done with molecules with over 800  atoms as well and it still works so  composite systems can have interference  another great question I got a lot was  what about gravity does that count as a  measurement to  answer this I'd need to assume just  slightly more quantum mechanics than  I've taught you so I'm gonna set this as  a homework question in the third main  video of this series and you guys can  give it a go for yourselves then and  I'll give you my solution in another one  of these videos but getting back to the  actual homework questions now the second  question was imagine if you had some  measuring device that could actually  measure which door an electron went  through what would happen remember I'd  said that electrons acts strangely when  they're not being measured but right now  it is being measured and so it has to  act normal again for an electron that  means that it has to be in just one  place you won't see it go through both  slits just the one but doesn't this  contradict what I'd said previously  since now we know each electron is going  through just one door  no because now the pattern on the wall  is no longer the interference pattern  instead it's exactly what you'd expect  in the case each electron goes through  one door which is what's happening in  this case quantum mechanics is sneaky  question three this one's about bohmian  mechanics and I'll keep it short because  I've talked myself hoarse about this  serie before the question said in  biomechanics aka pilot-wave theory the  electron does in fact go through just  one door in the double slit experiment  but in my last video I seemed to rule  out that possibility  so what incorrect assumption did I make  when we looked at the single slit  experiment we saw electrons go vaguely  here so we assumed that if electrons  were really going through one door each  an electron going through door one  wouldn't mind whether the other door  happens to be open and it would just do  what it was going to do anyway and end  up vaguely here in any case that's how  we got a contradiction but that's not  the case in bowie mechanics though the  electron does go through this one door  but it cares a lot about whether the  other door is open that's because it has  this thing called a guiding wave that  spreads through all of space and tells  the electron which way to go so when  both doors are open the wave goes  through both doors and then tells the  electron to go somewhere else  okay so those were the main homework  questions let's have a lightning round  of responses to other common comments  last doesn't the single slip pattern  actually look like this with one main  band and a few fainter bands on either  side whereas I always draw it as this  the answer is yes I just draw the main  chunk because it's easier and clearer  but I could have done it properly and  nothing would have changed in any of my  arguments next there were lots of people  who try to answer homework question one  by using this equation called the de  Broglie wavelength I made a video about  the uses and abuses of that equation but  basically that equation is only valid in  very very specific circumstances and one  that almost certainly doesn't apply to a  complicated real-world object  also I got a lot of comments saying that  I was wrong about waves because of  quantum field theory or that I should  talk about quantum field theory instead  because all the paradoxes clear up when  you look at it  actually I think the paradoxes of  quantum mechanics just get compounded in  qft but that's something to talk about  much much later  and that's all for this video thank you  so much to everyone who did leave a  comment on my last video and I really  encourage all of you to give the  homework questions ago in the future but  unfortunately while I love reading and  replying to all the comments soon it  will be too much for me to reply to each  one personally I'll keep reading them  all and I'll reply to as many as I can  in the future but that's where I'd love  for all of you to help me and comment on  each other's work as I saw in the  comments last time that leads to way  more interesting discussions anyway I  try to ask questions that can be  answered in multiple ways so you guys  can learn from each other and this works  when we all treat each other with  respect and remember that we're all here  to learn thanks guys see you for the  second proper video in this series  you

